- How would you handle SSR compatibility with this gate?
- What metrics or Lighthouse CI steps ensure the budget stays intact across deployments?

## Why This Is Asked
Broadcom needs engineers who can build robust real-time systems that work offline and handle network failures gracefully.

## Expected Answer
A strong candidate would discuss:
- Service worker caching strategies (network-first for API, cache-first for assets)
- IndexedDB for offline document storage
- WebSocket connection management with exponential backoff
- Conflict resolution using Operational Transform or CRDTs
- Background sync API for automatic data upload

## Code Example
```javascript
// Service worker with offline sync
self.addEventListener('sync', (event) => {
  if (event.tag === 'document-sync') {
    event.waitUntil(syncDocuments());
  }
});

async function syncDocuments() {
  const docs = await getOfflineDocuments();
  for (const doc of docs) {
    try {
      await fetch('/api/sync', {
        method: 'POST',
        body: JSON.stringify(doc)
      });
      await removeOfflineDoc(doc.id);
    } catch (err) {
      // Retry on next sync
      throw err;
    }
  }
}

// WebSocket reconnection with exponential backoff
class WebSocketManager {
  constructor(url) {
    this.url = url;
    this.reconnectAttempts = 0;
    this.maxReconnectAttempts = 5;
    this.baseDelay = 1000;
  }

  connect() {
    this.ws = new WebSocket(this.url);
    this.ws.onclose = () => this.handleReconnect();
  }

  handleReconnect() {
    if (this.reconnectAttempts < this.maxReconnectAttempts) {
      const delay = this.baseDelay * Math.pow(2, this.reconnectAttempts);
      setTimeout(() => {
        this.reconnectAttempts++;
        this.connect();
      }, delay);
    }
  }
}
```

## Concept Overview
A custom useDebounce hook defers function execution until a specified delay period has elapsed, making it essential for optimizing search inputs, API calls, and other performance-critical operations in React applications.

## Implementation Details
- Utilize useRef to maintain the latest callback reference, preventing stale closures across renders
- Implement comprehensive cleanup logic in useEffect to cancel pending debounced invocations
- Return a useCallback wrapper with stable dependencies for optimal concurrent rendering performance
- Handle component unmounting scenarios to prevent memory leaks and unexpected behavior
- Ensure proper cleanup of timers to maintain application stability

## Code Example
```javascript
import { useCallback, useRef, useEffect } from 'react';

export function useDebounce(callback, delay) {
  const callbackRef = useRef(callback);
  const timeoutRef = useRef(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    callbackRef.current = callback;
  }, [callback]);

  useEffect(() => {
    return () => {
      if (timeoutRef.current) {
        clearTimeout(timeoutRef.current);
      }
    };
  }, []);

  return useCallback((...args) => {
    if (timeoutRef.current) {
      clearTimeout(timeoutRef.current);
    }

    timeoutRef.current = setTimeout(() => {
      callbackRef.current(...args);
    }, delay);
  }, [delay]);
}
```

## Key Benefits
- Prevents stale closures through proper ref management
- Ensures cleanup on component unmount
- Maintains stable reference for concurrent rendering
- Handles edge cases and prevents memory leaks

